<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charlotte, Grand Duchess of Luxembourg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1896–1985</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Grand Duchess Charlotte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charlotte was Grand Duchess of Luxembourg from 1919 to 1964, succeeding her sister Marie-Adélaïde, who abdicated after being accused of pro-German conduct. A referendum in 1919 confirmed the monarchy under her. During the German occupation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> she led the government in exile from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and broadcast to her people, becoming a symbol of resistance. She abdicated in favour of her son Jean.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlotte-grand-duchess-of-luxembourg.jpg" alt="Charlotte, Grand Duchess of Luxembourg" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arnott &amp;amp; Rogers, Montreal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlotte,_Grand_Duchess_of_Luxembourg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
